WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.099
- [Narrator] Thanks to Mack
Weldon for keeping Legal Eagle

00:00:02.099 --> 00:00:04.789
in the air and helping me look fly.

00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:06.027
(eagle screeches)

00:00:06.027 --> 00:00:07.847
"Imagine being a layer,
skimming through old records

00:00:07.847 --> 00:00:09.867
"for months, working on a
strategy to free your clients

00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:13.397
"and this mo (beep) shows
up to court like this."

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:15.884
You can't fix stupid.

00:00:15.884 --> 00:00:18.467
(upbeat music)

00:00:20.006 --> 00:00:22.320
Hey Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.900
because I am outside of the eagle's lair

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.230
in a secret location that
may or not be related to

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.580
some skiing and snowboarding
that I am currently doing

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.970
and it's secret not
because it's a true secret,

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.760
but maybe because I don't want
people to judge me based on

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.110
my skiing and snowboarding skills,

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.630
but because I am on location,
I asked you to provide me

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.150
with some great legal
memes, and once again,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.520
you guys did not disappoint.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.410
I have so many legal memes to get through.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.850
We're gonna be laughing and crying

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.270
and everything in between.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:52.140
So without further ado,
let's dig in once again

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.476
to meme review.

00:00:53.476 --> 00:00:55.540
(rhythmic noise)

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.110
Meme Review!

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.710
Meme Review Trademark of
PewDiePie. All rights reserved.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.490
All right, so let's
dig into our first meme

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.067
that you guys sent in.

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:05.167
"The look you get when
opposing counsel is approaching

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.387
"the well without permission."

00:01:06.387 --> 00:01:07.510
(laughs)

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.110
So, as you know, you are not
allowed to enter the well

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:10.943
without permission.

00:01:10.943 --> 00:01:12.860
The well is the distance
between counsel table

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.690
and the judge, and if you are
overly aggressive about it,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.380
the bailiff will tackle you.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.213
Don't do it!

00:01:18.213 --> 00:01:19.239
Get permission first.

00:01:19.239 --> 00:01:21.237
(rhythmic noise)

00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:24.880
"When the only case you can
find to cite is from 1823."

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.270
True story, this happens
all the time, and yeah,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.640
we definitely feel like we
are in Victorian England here

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.430
with the powdered wigs and
the ridiculous ensembles.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:37.490
The thing is the older
cases are easier facts

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.640
and the opinions that judges
write were way easier to

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.990
understand a hundred or
two hundred years ago

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:43.823
than they are now.

00:01:43.823 --> 00:01:46.020
Now, if you get to court and
the appellate court writes

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.080
a decision, it's on a
very, very marginal issue

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.590
that's gonna be really
splitting some fine hairs,

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.310
so often, the really, really
old cases are often the best,

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.680
and of course, we lawyers feel like we are

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.192
characters in Amadeus.

00:01:59.192 --> 00:02:01.270
(rhythmic noise)

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.760
Okay, "when a first-year
redacts discovery."

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.890
A blurred face, another
blurred face, and the woman

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.980
whose face was blurred before.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.640
Ugh, first-year associates are the worst.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.840
When you graduate from law
school, you then often go to work

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.950
for a big firm, and when that
happens, the thing you realize

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.020
is that law school didn't
prepare you for anything.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.830
So, you are really being an
apprentice for a law firm,

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:25.970
and guess what?

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.792
You're gonna make some
mistakes, like redacting things

00:02:27.792 --> 00:02:29.850
that are obvious and
then forgetting to redact

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.357
the exact same thing later on.

00:02:31.357 --> 00:02:34.390
So yeah, first-years,
definitely double check

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:35.223
your redactions.

00:02:35.223 --> 00:02:37.317
(rhythmic noise)

00:02:37.317 --> 00:02:38.787
"When your client tells you he's done

00:02:38.787 --> 00:02:40.120
"his own legal research."

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.270
(laughs)

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:44.320
This happened to me a couple of days ago.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.940
It wasn't a client so much as a potential

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.220
opposing party who had clearly
done some legal research

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.560
on their own by going on Google,
which is the equivalent of

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.400
going on WebMD and then
diagnosing yourself with cancer.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.500
Do not do your own legal research.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.610
You will be wrong.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.042
You will look like an
incredible idiot to anyone that

00:03:02.042 --> 00:03:03.840
actually knows what they're doing.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.119
Friends don't let friends
do their own legal research.

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.037
(rhythmic noise)

00:03:08.037 --> 00:03:10.477
"When someone tells me
they got into law school:

00:03:10.477 --> 00:03:12.390
"My deepest sympathies."

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.210
Oh, man. Attorney Problems is
really knocking it out here.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:16.880
Law school can be a lot of fun.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.230
Law school can also be
very, very terrible,

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.231
and like the video that
I did last week about

00:03:21.231 --> 00:03:24.230
my crazy ex-girlfriend, a
lot of people really, really

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.970
hate it, and they say,
"Don't be a lawyer."

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.430
I am not necessarily one of those people.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.368
Some people should not go to
law school, but some people

00:03:29.368 --> 00:03:31.400
definitely should go to law school.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.640
So, if you go to law school,
you have my sympathies,

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.749
but there are ways to get through it.

00:03:35.749 --> 00:03:37.806
(rhythmic noise)

00:03:37.806 --> 00:03:40.537
"Um, yeah. If you could
stop calling every other day

00:03:40.537 --> 00:03:42.526
"for a case update, that'd be great."

00:03:42.526 --> 00:03:43.440
(laughs)

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.750
So I think this is about
a client that wants

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:46.583
too many updates.

00:03:46.583 --> 00:03:48.400
I don't really mind that.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.760
The clients need to be informed,
and frankly, it's the duty

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.181
of the lawyer to reach
out to their client and

00:03:52.181 --> 00:03:55.518
give them updates, so, often
lawyers don't give updates

00:03:55.518 --> 00:03:56.795
nearly enough.

00:03:56.795 --> 00:04:00.528
But yeah, they can definitely
ask for too many updates.

00:04:00.528 --> 00:04:02.567
(rhythmic noise)

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:05.267
"Me as a lawyer: my client is
innocent. Them: your client is

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:08.207
"on video dragging a
lifeless body into the woods.

00:04:08.207 --> 00:04:09.426
"I said what I said."

00:04:09.426 --> 00:04:10.450
(laughs)

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.760
You know, it's not so much
video that is the thing that

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.270
really kills clients, at
least in a civil context.

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.320
That definitely does happen
in a criminal context.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.130
But in a civil context,
which is most of what I do,

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.640
it's really the emails.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.360
The emails will kill
you every single time.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.980
People don't understand that
those emails are going to be

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:30.120
used against you in a court of law.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.905
The lawyers are basically stuck with

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:34.200
arguing what we have to argue.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.113
I said what I said.

00:04:35.113 --> 00:04:37.097
(rhythmic noise)

00:04:37.097 --> 00:04:39.370
"When the old dude asks if
you're the court reporter."

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.960
Oh, oh, that's bad, yeah.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.040
Law often can be an old
boys' club and they will make

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.810
all kinds of really sexist
remarks, and it's a problem

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:53.060
for female young associates
especially that they get

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.850
mistaken for court reporters
or staff or something else.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.570
Times will change eventually
and people will understand that

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.969
that's not appropriate,
but it definitely happens.

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.267
(rhythmic noise)

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.527
"The sales contract had
a few strings attached.

00:05:07.527 --> 00:05:09.168
"I played with them for 2 hours."

00:05:09.168 --> 00:05:10.336
(laughs)

00:05:10.336 --> 00:05:11.204
Aw, kitty.

00:05:11.204 --> 00:05:12.166
(cat meows)

00:05:12.166 --> 00:05:14.230
I don't think this needs
any explanation from me.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.693
This is just a perfect joke.

00:05:15.693 --> 00:05:17.637
(rhythmic noise)

00:05:17.637 --> 00:05:19.787
"When you're a law student
that hasn't argued with anyone

00:05:19.787 --> 00:05:21.987
"for a few seconds: I
will argue with anyone

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:22.820
"about anything."

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:24.240
(laughs)

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.120
Okay, yeah, law students have
a well-deserved reputation

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.020
for arguing a lot.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.474
In fact, there's actually a term for that.

00:05:30.474 --> 00:05:34.620
The person or people that
have to argue all the time,

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.700
they're called gunners, and we hate them.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:37.890
They just can't shut up.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.680
They have to have the last word.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:40.810
They have to ask questions.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.210
Yeah, they will argue
about anything with anyone.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:45.070
They're terrible people.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:45.903
Don't be a gunner.

00:05:45.903 --> 00:05:47.837
(rhythmic noise)

00:05:47.837 --> 00:05:50.840
"Civil law in a nutshell.
General Rule. Exception."

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.690
Oh my god. Oh, that really hits home.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:55.640
For example, we've talked
about hearsay a lot

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:56.810
on this channel.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:59.627
As you probably know, there are
a lot of hearsay exceptions,

00:05:59.627 --> 00:06:03.196
and as you probably know, the
exceptions to the hearsay rule

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:06.600
swallow the hearsay rule in general.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.850
Most things that are considered
hearsay will come in.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.240
Like this dam, the dam is
broken, and all of that stuff

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.840
is coming in, and frankly,
I don't think that's

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.030
the worst thing.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:18.225
Civil law, especially civil
procedure, man, the exceptions

00:06:18.225 --> 00:06:21.998
really preponderate over the general rule.

00:06:21.998 --> 00:06:24.017
(rhythmic noise)

00:06:24.017 --> 00:06:26.737
"Courtroom exchange of the day.
Doctor, before you performed

00:06:26.737 --> 00:06:28.367
"the autopsy, did you check for a pulse?

00:06:28.367 --> 00:06:29.200
"No.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.117
"Did you check for blood pressure?

00:06:30.117 --> 00:06:30.950
"No.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:31.783
"Did you check for breathing?

00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:32.616
"No.

00:06:32.616 --> 00:06:34.437
"So, then is it possible
that the patient was alive

00:06:34.437 --> 00:06:35.587
"when you begin the autopsy?

00:06:35.587 --> 00:06:36.420
"No.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:37.787
"How can you be sure, doctor?

00:06:37.787 --> 00:06:40.387
"Because his brain was
sitting on my desk in a jar.

00:06:40.387 --> 00:06:43.077
"But could the patient have
still been alive nevertheless?

00:06:43.077 --> 00:06:44.857
"Yes, it is possible that
he could have been alive

00:06:44.857 --> 00:06:45.930
"and practicing law."

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.260
(laughs)

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.630
I definitely have seen this one before.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.500
It's been floating around
the internet for a long time.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.970
I would love for someone to
track down to see if this is

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.070
actually a real deposition transcript.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.690
It's just too good to be true.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.473
I really want it to be true.

00:07:02.473 --> 00:07:04.160
I kind of doubt that it is.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.200
And also, I don't think that
an autopsy would have begun

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:08.780
with a brain in a jar.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.210
I think you would have to
perform the autopsy for

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:12.410
their brain to be removed.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.540
If only there was some sort
of medical professional

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:18.220
that could let me know how
autopsies are done, that would

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.635
be really helpful in this situation.

00:07:19.635 --> 00:07:22.078
- [man] Hey, Legal Eagle.
Doctor Francis here.

00:07:22.078 --> 00:07:25.193
Now, I'm somewhat dubious
about this particular meme

00:07:25.193 --> 00:07:27.850
and I'd like to present
the following evidence

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.450
for your consideration.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:33.230
You see an autopsy or
post-mortem normally begins after

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:36.280
an external examination
of the body by making

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.516
a large incision from
the neck all the way down

00:07:38.516 --> 00:07:40.580
to the pelvic bone.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.240
Sometimes it's Y-shaped,
starting at either shoulder and

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:46.120
meeting around the ziphy
sternum and again continuing

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.200
down to the pelvis.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.410
At that point, all the internal
organs of the chest and the

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:53.810
abdomen are removed and inspected.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.030
Only at that point is the
skull cavity opened and

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:00.180
the brain removed, so I
find it hard to believe that

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.985
the brain would have been
on the pathologist's desk

00:08:01.985 --> 00:08:04.550
at the start of the procedure.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.930
Hence, I submit to you thus,
that this joke is guilty of

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.480
medical meme malpractice.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.026
- [Narrator] Thanks,
Rohin. I learned something.

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:15.827
(rhythmic noise)

00:08:15.827 --> 00:08:18.427
"I think I found my new
lawyer. Just because you did it

00:08:18.427 --> 00:08:19.614
"doesn't mean you're guilty."

00:08:19.614 --> 00:08:20.625
(laughs)

00:08:20.625 --> 00:08:22.620
That's actually true, believe it or not.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.330
So for example, let's say that a person

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:26.540
kills another person.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.798
You would think that that
would be murder, but let's say

00:08:28.798 --> 00:08:30.360
that they acted in self-defense.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.820
Just because you killed
someone, you "did it",

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.890
doesn't necessarily mean
that you're guilty of

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.350
the crime of murder.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.710
So yeah, this lawyer, he knows what's up.

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:40.677
(rhythmic noise)

