00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.769
and you find out it's pretty freaking bad.

00:09:22.769 --> 00:09:24.058
(laughs)

00:09:24.058 --> 00:09:25.623
- [Women] Turn around!
Turn this bus right around!

00:09:28.716 --> 00:09:30.637
(rhythmic noise)

00:09:30.637 --> 00:09:32.407
- [Narrator] "Sprinkling your
law school essay with Latin

00:09:32.407 --> 00:09:34.870
"legal terminologies like Non est factum,

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:37.743
Contra preferentem, Ratio decidendi"

00:09:38.966 --> 00:09:40.730
(laughs)

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:41.563
I can't even pronounce that.

00:09:41.563 --> 00:09:43.151
Ratio decen

00:09:43.151 --> 00:09:45.030
Decidendi.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:46.587
That's a new one by me.

00:09:46.587 --> 00:09:49.170
"Onus of proof. Obiter
dicta. Stare decisis."

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.330
Okay. Common misconception
about lawyers: good lawyers

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:56.420
really try to avoid adding
too much Latin terminology and

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.850
really any kind of inside
baseball legal terminologies.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.294
It's not persuasive.

00:10:01.294 --> 00:10:04.360
Sometimes, you have to do it,
but good legal writers will do

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.820
everything they can to avoid
adding the kind of language

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.763
that isn't going to resonate
with most people, so yeah.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.950
Law students like to add that
(bleep), but good lawyers

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:17.937
should generally not if they can avoid it.

00:10:17.937 --> 00:10:19.857
